"""LDhybrid v1.0 -- hybrid4 limb-darkening coefficients, MPS-ATLAS Set 1 (plane-parallel; Kostogryz et al. 2022), JWST_NIRSpec_PRISM passband.

Standalone: needs only numpy; the coefficient grid is embedded.

    from ldhybrid_hybrid4_mps1_nirspec_prism import coefficients, intensity
    r = coefficients(teff=5600, logg=4.4, mh=0.0)
    r["coeffs"]      # (w1, w2, w3, w4); also r["w"]
    r["q"]           # (q1, q2, q3, q4): sampling coordinates in [0, 1]
    r["physical"]    # positive and monotonic?
    intensity(r["coeffs"], mu)                  # I(mu)/I(1)
    from_q(q), to_q(c)                          # unit hypercube <-> coefficients

Law (hybrid4, curvature-simplex basis):
    I(mu)/I(1) = 1 - w1 (1 - mu^2) - w2 (1 - mu^2)^2 - w3 S3(mu) - w4 S4(mu)
    S3, S4 = B[k] . (1 - mu^4, (1 - mu^2)^2, P(mu; e0), P(mu; e1)), B = BASIS[2:]
    P(mu; e) = [(mu^2 + e)^-2 - (1 + e)^-2] / [e^-2 - (1 + e)^-2]
    e0, e1 = 0.028601207249486824, 0.38582417584735224  (pole ladder with gamma = 25/16)
With x = mu^2, w >= 0 and sum(w) <= 1 is exactly the set of hybrid4 profiles
with I(0) >= 0, dI/dx >= 0, d2I/dx2 <= 0, d3I/dx3 >= 0 on [0, 1]. Sampling
coordinates (the analogue of Kipping 2013's q1, q2): s = sum(w) = 1 - I(0) =
q1^(1/4), w1 = s(1 - q2^(1/3)), w2 = s q2^(1/3)(1 - q3^(1/2)),
w3 = s q2^(1/3) q3^(1/2)(1 - q4), w4 = s q2^(1/3) q3^(1/2) q4; uniform q is a
uniform prior over the region. Weights on SquishierPlanet's monomial-pole
hybrid4 shapes: w @ BASIS.

Coefficients: least squares uniform in projected radius (the transit-optimised
fit), constrained to that region. Grid: 16974 models, Teff 3500-7500 K,
log g 3.5-5.0, [M/H] -2.0 to +1.0.
Interpolation is linear on the Kuhn simplices of the (Teff, log g, [M/H])
tensor grid, as in ldhybrid.interpolate: continuous, exact at the models, and
a convex mix of four models (the law's physical region is convex, so a mix of
physical models is physical).
Outside the grid, coefficients() raises unless extrapolate=True (the nearest
model in Teff/100 K, log g/0.5, [M/H]/0.5).
lc_ppm is the light-curve error of the tabulated fits (p = 0.1).

Generated by LDhybrid scripts/make_standalone.py from the v1.0 CDS tables.
Do not use with hybrid4 implementations that use the old alpha = 1 poles
(0.01176, 0.3041), or that take monomial-pole weights without converting.
"""

